Hi, 

In the versions of HikariCP starting from 6.0.0 (all 6.x.x versions), the `java.sql.Connection` interface contract is violated for the `getSchema()` in my opinion. The method contract says

`Returns: the current schema name or null if there is none`

Below is a very simple code that can replicate the difference - 

```
HikariConfig config = new HikariConfig();
        config.setJdbcUrl("jdbc:postgresql://localhost:5432/test"); // Replace with your DB URL
        config.setUsername("test"); // Replace with your DB username
        config.setPassword("test"); // Replace with your DB password
        config.setMaximumPoolSize(10);

        try (
                HikariDataSource dataSource = new HikariDataSource(config)) {
            // Get a connection from the pool
            try (Connection connection = dataSource.getConnection()) {
                // Set correct schema before proceeding
                connection.setSchema("invalid");
                if (Objects.isNull(connection.getSchema())) {
                    log.info("Connection contract adhered to.");
                } else {
                    log.error("Connection contract violated.");
                }
            }
        } catch (Exception exception) {
            log.error("An error occurred while connecting to the database", exception);
        }
```

Previously, the call to `getSchema()` was delegated, but is now handled in the `ProxyConnection` and delegated only in the case it was dirtied? Version 5.1.0 delegates the call correctly. In our case, we are using postgres underneath (so, `PgConnection` is checking if the schema actually exists and returns a null when the schema does not exist). 

Is this a correct conclusion? If so, would it just mean that we remove the `getSchema()` method from the `ProxyConnection` introduced in https://github.com/brettwooldridge/HikariCP/pull/2199/files

Best regards,
Adarsh

### Context
HikariCP:3.4.5
postgres-jdbc:42.2.14
spring-boot:2.1.16
jooq:3.13.4

### Symptom
I wanted to change my Jooq configuration to not have to insert schema identifiers in the SQL it generates, so I told Jooq not to do that any more and added the `schema` property to my HikariConfig (I configure using code).

That seemed to work, but then intermittently, I would get postgres "relation ... does not exist" errors.


### Possible root cause
`com.zaxxer.hikari.pool.ProxyConnection.close()` is doing a rollback (because the connection `!isAutoCommit`) which is resetting the postgres `SESSION search_path` (see [PgConnection.setSchema(String)](https://github.com/pgjdbc/pgjdbc/blob/529e5dc3a8f071ad4946daf3bde1422c21a99524/pgjdbc/src/main/java/org/postgresql/jdbc/PgConnection.java#L1626)).
But, as shown below - the `close()` method is not setting the `DIRTY_BIT_SCHEMA`.  It appears the connection search path does not get reset the next time that connection is checked out.

### Evidence
I set a break-point in my IDE on the `delegate.rollback()` line in the `ProxyConnection.close()` method as given below.
Before call to `rollback()`, if I called `PgConnection.getSchema()` it would return my custom schema name, after the call, it returned `public` - so I'm assuming a postgres `rollback` statement also applies to the postgres `session` on the server that the JDBC connection is attached to.
 
com.zaxxer.hikari.pool.ProxyConnection:
```
   public final void close() throws SQLException
   {
      // Closing statements can cause connection eviction, so this must run before the conditional below
      closeStatements();

      if (delegate != ClosedConnection.CLOSED_CONNECTION) {
         leakTask.cancel();

         try {
            if (isCommitStateDirty && !isAutoCommit) {
               delegate.rollback();
               lastAccess = currentTime();
               LOGGER.debug("{} - Executed rollback on connection {} due to dirty commit state on close().", poolEntry.getPoolName(), delegate);
            }

            if (dirtyBits != 0) {
               poolEntry.resetConnectionState(this, dirtyBits);
               lastAccess = currentTime();
            }

            delegate.clearWarnings();
         }
         catch (SQLException e) {
            // when connections are aborted, exceptions are often thrown that should not reach the application
            if (!poolEntry.isMarkedEvicted()) {
               throw checkException(e);
            }
         }
         finally {
            delegate = ClosedConnection.CLOSED_CONNECTION;
            poolEntry.recycle(lastAccess);
         }
      }
   }
```

### Workaround
My current workaround is append the `currentSchema` param to my postgres jdbc url, as in: `jdbc:postgresql://localhost/postgres?currentSchema=api_svc`

I'm thinking I'm going to stick with this workaround since, given the underlying implementation of `PgConnection.setSchema()`, using the Hikari `schema` property results in the extra JDBC `executeUpdate()` to set the search path.

Hello, I am running a play 2.4.6 with HikariCP 2.4.7 and Hibernate in production. I've been having this problem for a while, but not sure why this happens every two or three days.

My server hangs when it is unable to acquire JDBC connection. Once this exception is thrown, the usage of CPU grows up to 70~80% and it hangs the application in the end. In addition to this, when I enable leak leakDetectionThreshold to 2000ms, it shows warnings quite often. So, I assumed that my code doesn't do db calls properly.

I've attached code blocks and stack trace. I would really appreciate if someone could point out what I did wrong when I made database calls. Thank you in advance.

```
import java.util.function.Function;
import javax.persistence.EntityManager;
import javax.persistence.EntityTransaction;
